RFC: value: share list payloads on copy, materialize on mutation

Copying a GST_TYPE_LIST / GST_TYPE_ARRAY / unique-list GValue deep-copied
every entry: a GValue init plus a heap string copy per entry for the
format lists all negotiation caps carry. Caps are conceptually immutable
once created, yet every gst_caps_copy, fixate and query-path copy paid
the full walk.

Give the private GstValueList an atomic refcount. Copies share the
payload, and the mutation entry points (append, prepend, and the
entry-stealing gst_value_list_concat_and_take_values) materialize a
private copy first when it is shared. The GArray-prefix layout that
gststructure.c peeks at is preserved (the refcount sits after the
existing fields).

Semantics notes for review:

- NOCOPY values borrow a caller-owned list. They are never shared into
  (copy from a NOCOPY source still deep-copies) and mutate their
  borrowed payload in place exactly as before.
- Mutating list entries through a peeked or const-cast pointer was
  always undefined. It previously corrupted one value, it now corrupts
  every sharer. This is the main behavioral risk of the change.
- Concurrent copy/free of DIFFERENT values sharing a payload is safe
  via the atomic refcount. Concurrent copy and mutation of the SAME
  value was never allowed and still is not.

gstcapsops benchmark, best of 5, on top of the two previous commits
(videoconvert-style template, 82-entry format lists):

  copy template     11700 ->  785 ns  (14.9x)
  fixate template   12234 ->  986 ns  (12.4x)

All 113 core tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

